1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,801
If your stomach feels tight and bloated after eating, even on a day you ate clean, the real culprit might be one of these 3 everyday habits, not your last meal.

2
00:00:11,169 --> 00:00:24,620
Sugar alcohols like sorbitol, maltitol, and xylitol in protein bars and shakes barely get absorbed, so gut bacteria ferment them in your colon and produce gas.

3
00:00:24,988 --> 00:00:31,991
Look for bars sweetened with monk fruit or stevia instead, and check the label for sugar alcohol grams.

4
00:00:32,359 --> 00:00:43,768
Raw broccoli, kale, cabbage, and Brussels sprouts are loaded with raffinose, a sugar your gut can't break down until bacteria ferment it in the colon.

5
00:00:44,136 --> 00:00:54,740
Steaming them instead of eating them raw makes them much easier for your body to break down, so you end up with far less gas than eating them raw.

6
00:00:55,108 --> 00:01:05,031
Sipping iced coffee or a smoothie through a straw pulls extra air into your stomach with every sip, a habit doctors call aerophagia.

7
00:01:05,399 --> 00:01:10,020
Drinking straight from the cup instead keeps that extra air out.
